3. THIRD-PARTY SOURCE  —  NOT LICENSED HERE.  READ THIS BEFORE REDISTRIBUTING.
================================================================================

**This licence does not grant you any right to the source code of the audited
protocols, and it cannot.**

The dataset quotes Solidity source from eight public Code4rena audits. Those
repositories are licensed by their authors, not by us. Most declare **no
licence at all**; several are BUSL-1.1 or carry the Commons Clause. Quotation for
research and citation is a different question from redistribution, and that
question is the downstream user's to answer — we cannot answer it for you and we
have not obtained permission from those authors.

Concretely, as measured by `scripts/audit_third_party.py` and recorded per record
in `derived/records.jsonl`:

  * records whose `redistribution.flag` is
    `source_redistributed_without_declared_license` contain verbatim source text
    quoted from a repository that declares no licence. **If you intend to
    redistribute this dataset, or to train a model on it and ship the model or
    its outputs, you must decide for yourself whether that use is lawful in your
    jurisdiction. We make no representation that it is.**
  * records whose `redistribution.flag` is `withheld` contain **no** source text.
    The citation survives: path, commit, line range, URL and sha256.
    `scripts/fetch_sources.py` retrieves the file locally so its structure can be
    analysed without copying it out of the repository.
  * PoC code in `jsonl/exploits.jsonl` belongs to the Code4rena submission
    authors. It is redistributed as quoted evidence, with its original SPDX
    identifier preserved where the author supplied one, and with **no** licence
    applied by us. `attribution` in `NOTICE.md` names the authors.
  * Harness code the VLUN authors wrote for `experiments/` is the authors' own
    work and carries `SPDX-License-Identifier: BUSL-1.1`, which is a
    source-available licence, **not** an OSI open-source licence and **not**
    covered by the MIT grant in part 2. It converts to MIT after the Change Date
    defined in BUSL-1.1; until then, treat it as BUSL-1.1.

What we have done, and what we have not:

  DONE    every quoted citation is pinned to an exact commit and carries a sha256,
          so any recipient can identify precisely which bytes were quoted;
  DONE    every record states, in `license_classification` and `redistribution`,
          what the upstream repository declared and whether text was included;
  DONE    some citations were withheld entirely rather than quoted;
  NOT DONE  we have not obtained redistribution permission from the upstream
          protocol authors, and we cannot.

If you need a clean dataset for redistribution or commercial training, use the
withheld records, or use `scripts/fetch_sources.py` to pull sources yourself
under your own compliance process.

